Student-Friendly Study Planner: A Python-runned Time Management Tool for Students!

1. Problem Statement

Many students today struggle to balance multiple academic responsibilities: Assignments, quizzes, and projects. This is due to poor time management and unorganized study routines.
In a typical school setting, students often face difficulties deciding which task to prioritize, resulting in missed deadlines, overlapping workloads, and academic stress. 
This problem is especially common among juniors like me who are still developing learning habits.

The Study Planner project addresses this issue by creating a rule-driven scheduling tool that helps students organize and prioritize their academic tasks systematically.
Instead of relying on memory or manual lists, this project will:

-- Guide students in inputting and saving their study tasks (such as homework, projects, and reviews).

-- Automatically generate a logical schedule based on due dates and priority levels.

-- Help users track task completion and maintain a sense of academic progress.

By simplifying task management and applying a clear prioritization system, this project aims to reduce confusion, improve productivity, and promote responsible study habits among students.

** A rule-driven scheduling tool is a function in my code that organizes and prioritizes tasks automatically by following a specific set of logical rules or conditions.
An example of these logical rules are the tasks "Due Date" and "Priority Level". **

2. Project Objectives

-- To develop a Python-based study planner that allows users to input, store, and retrieve their academic tasks in an organized way.

-- To implement a rule-based scheduling function that automatically prioritizes tasks based on due date proximity and priority level, helping students decide which to complete first.

-- To provide a simple, text-based user interface that ensures accessibility and usability without requiring external databases or graphical tools.

-- To enable data persistence through simple text file storage, allowing students to save, update, and review their schedules conveniently.

-- To promote effective time management habits by giving students a structured, logic-based method for planning and tracking their academic workload.


3. Planned Features

-- Task Storage System:

All tasks will be stored in a single text file (tasks.txt) to ensure simplicity and accessibility.

This feature solves the problem of scattered or lost information by centralizing all study tasks in one place.

-- Task Input and Management:

The program will prompt users to enter task details such as:

ID (unique identifier)

= Subject

= Due Date (YYYY-MM-DD)

= Estimated Time 

= Priority (1 = High, 3 = Low)

= Status (Pending or Completed)

This structured input format ensures that users record tasks systematically, helping reduce confusion and missed deadlines.

-- Automated Schedule Generation:

The system will apply a rule-based sorting function that arranges tasks first by due date and then by priority.

This addresses the issue of not knowing which task to start first by presenting a clear, automatically organized schedule.

Task Completion Update Feature:

Users can mark tasks as “Completed,” allowing them to monitor progress and maintain motivation when they're tired.

This supports accountability and helps students track how well they are managing their workload.

-- User-Friendly Console Interface:

All interactions occur through simple prompts using input() and print() functions, making the program easy to use even for beginners.

This ensures accessibility for all users without requiring additional installations or advanced technical knowledge.

-- Modifying Tasks:

Users can update existing tasks by entering the task ID and modifying specific details such as the subject, due date, estimated time, priority, or status.

This feature addresses real-world scenarios where schedules change — allowing students to adjust their plans without deleting or re-entering tasks.
It improves flexibility, accuracy, and helps maintain an up-to-date planner.

-- Rule-based scheduling system:

The Rule-Based Scheduling System is the core logic that determines how the tasks are organized within the planner.
It follows a set of predefined rules to ensure fairness, efficiency, and consistency when sorting tasks:

= Due Date Rule – Tasks with earlier deadlines are placed first.

= Priority Rule – Among tasks with the same due date, those with higher importance (priority level 1) appear before others.

This system uses Python’s built-in sorting capabilities with a custom rule function that interprets these conditions.

4. Planned Inputs and Outputs

Inputs:

Task details (subject, due date, estimated time, priority, and status).

User commands (e.g., Add Task, View Schedule, Mark Task as Completed).

When the user chooses “Add Task”, the program prompts for the following details:

ex. Output

Enter Task ID: 1
Enter Subject: English Quiz Review
Enter Due Date (YYYY-MM-DD): 2025-11-20
Enter Estimated Time (hours): 3
Enter Priority (1 = High, 3 = Low): 1
Enter Status (Pending/Completed): Pending

When the user selects “View Schedule”, the program reads the text file and displays all tasks in their current order:

ex. Output

ID | Subject             | Due Date   | Hours | Priority | Status
---------------------------------------------------------------
2  | CS2 Long Quiz Review | 2025-11-15 | 1     | 2         | Pending
3  | Algebra Homework     | 2025-11-18 | 2     | 1         | Completed
1  | English Quiz Review  | 2025-11-20 | 3     | 1         | Pending

Tasks are automatically sorted by due date (earliest first) and priority level (1 = highest). However, it leans heavier into due date rather than priority level.

If the user wants to modify a task (for example, change the status of Task 2 to “Completed”), the system prompts:

ex. Output

Enter the Task ID you want to modify: 2
Enter the new status (Pending/Completed): Completed
Task updated successfully!

After modification, the updated schedule is displayed:

ID | Subject             | Due Date   | Hours | Priority | Status
---------------------------------------------------------------
2  | CS2 Long Quiz Review | 2025-11-15 | 1     | 2         | Completed
3  | Algebra Homework     | 2025-11-18 | 2     | 1         | Completed
1  | English Quiz Review  | 2025-11-20 | 3     | 1         | Pending

5. Logic Plan

START PROGRAM
DISPLAY "Welcome to the Student-Friendly Study Planner!"

LOOP until user chooses to exit:
    DISPLAY:
        1 - Add Task
        2 - View Schedule
        3 - Modify Task
        4 - Mark Task as Completed
        5 - Exit

    PROMPT user for choice → choice

    IF choice == 1 THEN
        PROMPT for task details (ID, Subject, Due Date, Hours, Priority, Status)
        FORMAT task data as "ID|Subject|DueDate|Hours|Priority|Status"
        APPEND task line to "tasks.txt"
        DISPLAY "Task added successfully!"

    ELSE IF choice == 2 THEN
        OPEN "tasks.txt" and READ all lines
        CONVERT each line into task records
        SORT tasks using RULE-BASED SYSTEM:
            - First by Due Date (earliest first)
            - Then by Priority (1 = highest)
        DISPLAY formatted schedule (ID, Subject, Due Date, Hours, Priority, Status)

    ELSE IF choice == 3 THEN
        READ all tasks from "tasks.txt"
        PROMPT for Task ID to modify
        IF Task ID found THEN
            PROMPT which field to update
            PROMPT for new value
            UPDATE task data
            WRITE all updated tasks back to file
            DISPLAY "Task modified successfully!"
        ELSE
            DISPLAY "Task ID not found."

    ELSE IF choice == 4 THEN
        READ all tasks from "tasks.txt"
        PROMPT for Task ID to mark as completed
        IF Task ID found THEN
            CHANGE Status to "Completed"
            WRITE all updated tasks back to file
            DISPLAY "Task marked as completed!"
        ELSE
            DISPLAY "Task ID not found."

    ELSE IF choice == 5 THEN
        DISPLAY "Exiting program... Goodbye!"
        EXIT LOOP

    ELSE
        DISPLAY "Invalid choice. Please select 1-5."

END LOOP
END PROGRAM




